  "panel_source": "MAT-I (Materials)",
  "panel_reasoning_excerpt": "## Panel Charge\n\nThis panel addresses two closely related gaps identified in Document 33 (Dual-Index Cross-Reference Layer): **A1 Materials & Surfaces** (coverage \u2605\u2605 Partial) and **A7 Haptic & Thermal Properties** (coverage \u2605 Minimal). These domains are combined into a single panel because material properties are the primary source of haptic-thermal experience \u2014 you cannot discuss how architecture feels to the touch or to the body without discussing what it is made of.\n\nThe gap analysis found that visual and acoustic properties of materials are reasonably well-mapped to existing templates (T1 fractal fluency for wood grain, AX1/AX2 for acoustic absorption) but that haptic, thermal, and olfactory pathways are identified without being formalized. No dedicated template exists for somatosensory prediction error, thermal comfort as prediction, the affective touch pathway in architecture, or material-identity integration across modalities. Meanwhile, the key architectural question \u2014 *why does wood reduce stress?* (Doc 33, A1 open questions) \u2014 cannot be answered by the current template set because the answer is almost certainly multi-modal convergence, and the modalities beyond vision and acoustics lack mechanistic templates.\n\nAdditionally, Panel L-I (Light & Luminance, Doc 34) flagged the **light-material interaction** \u2014 how illumination reveals or transforms material appearance \u2014 as work that belongs in this panel. L1 (Luminance Contrast PE) and L4 (CCT as Ecological Signal) operate on whatever the visual system receives, and what it receives depends jointly on the light and the material. This panel must formalize that joint dependency.\n\nThe panel is charged with producing 4\u20136 mechanistic templates covering: (1) somatosensory prediction and haptic PE, (2) thermal comfort as allostatic prediction, (3) the C-tactile affective touch pathway in architecture, (4) multi-modal material-identity integration, (5) material-cultural conditioning and learned associations, and (6) the light-material interaction as a joint determinant of visual PE. These templates must connect to the existing infrastructure \u2014 particularly T1/T2 (visual complexity), T8 (affordance), T12 (interoceptive affect), T29 (allostatic regulation), AX5 (cross-modal congruence), L1 (luminance PE), and L4 (color rendering).\n\n---",
